# What never belongs in the image build context.
#
# Without this the context is ~385MB and, worse, `COPY backend/ ./backend/` in the build stage
# copies the HOST's node_modules over the ones `npm ci` just installed in the image. Those are built
# for the host's platform and architecture; on any cross-platform build (an arm64 laptop producing
# an amd64 image, which is the common case) the result is an image containing native binaries that
# cannot run. It works by accident today only because nothing in this tree ships a native addon.
#
# Everything the Dockerfile actually needs is named explicitly in it: the package manifests, the
# lockfile, backend/src, backend/migrations and backend/tsconfig*.json.

# Dependencies — installed inside the image, never copied into it.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build and test output. `dist` is produced by the build stage; a stale host copy must not shadow it.
dist
**/dist
coverage
**/coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# The pinned rundler binary (~54MB) fetched for the integration tests. Nothing in the image runs it.
backend/.bundler

# Foundry output and submodules. The backend image contains no contracts; artifacts are read by the
# TEST suite, which runs outside the image.
contracts/out
contracts/cache
contracts/lib
contracts/broadcast

# Not part of the service.
.git
.github
docs
deploy
sdk
*.md

# Local configuration and secrets. `.env` holds a signer key on a dev machine; it must never be
# baked into a layer, where it survives every later deletion.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
